'''Peter Andreas Thiel''' ({{IPAc-en|t|iː|l|audio=En-us-Thiel.ogg}}; born 11 October 1967) is a German-American<ref name="GAHF">{{cite web |date=2018 |title=Honoring Mr. Peter Thiel 2018 Distinguished German-American of the Year |url=https://gahmusa.org/wp-content/uploads/gala-program%202018.pdf |access-date=22 September 2025 |website=gahmusa.org |publisher=German-American Heritage Foundation of the USA |page=9 |quote=He has also always remembered his roots and kept strong ties to his home country – holding German citizenship and visiting Germany on a regular basis. Mr. Thiel is a true example of German-American friendship and cooperation.}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Goldberg |first1=Jonah |date=18 October 2022 |title=Goldberg: Why is Peter Thiel, a GOP megadonor, buying a Maltese passport? |url=https://www.latimes.com/opinion/story/2022-10-18/peter-thiel-malta-jd-vance-blake-masters |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221018100947/https://www.latimes.com/opinion/story/2022-10-18/peter-thiel-malta-jd-vance-blake-masters |archive-date=18 October 2022 |url-status=live |work=Los Angeles Times |quote=Born in Germany, he has dual U.S. and German citizenship.}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Peter Thiel's unusual path to New Zealand citizenship - CBS News |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/peter-thiels-unusual-path-to-new-zealand-citizenship/ |work=www.cbsnews.com |date=1 February 2017 |quote=The 2011 documents show that Thiel also remained a citizen of Germany, where he was born.}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Towey |first1=Hannah |title=Billionaire Silicon Valley investor Peter Thiel is in the process of acquiring Maltese citizenship, report says |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/peter-thiel-malta-golden-passport-citizenship-nyt-report-2022-10 |work=Business Insider |date=16 October 2022}}</ref> entrepreneur, [[venture capital]]ist, and [[Conservatism|conservative]] [[Political movement|political activist]].<ref name=":4">{{Cite magazine|last=Wiener|first=Anna|date=27 October 2021|title=What Is It About Peter Thiel?|url=https://www.newyorker.com/news/letter-from-silicon-valley/what-is-it-about-peter-thiel|access-date=7 February 2022|magazine=[[The New Yorker]]|language=en-US|archive-date=25 December 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211225143619/https://www.newyorker.com/news/letter-from-silicon-valley/what-is-it-about-peter-thiel|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Heffernan|first=Virginia|date=21 September 2021|title=The Alarming Rise of Peter Thiel, Tech Mogul and Political Provocateur|language=en-US|work=[[The New York Times]]|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/21/books/review/the-contrarian-peter-thiel-max-chafkin.html|access-date=7 February 2022|issn=0362-4331|archive-date=6 February 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220206130523/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/21/books/review/the-contrarian-peter-thiel-max-chafkin.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Purdy |first=Jedediah |title=The Anti-Democratic Worldview of Steve Bannon and Peter Thiel |url=https://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2016/11/donald-trump-steve-bannon-peter-thiel-214490/ |access-date=7 February 2022 |website=[[Politico Magazine]] |date=30 November 2016 |language=en |archive-date=6 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220206210948/https://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2016/11/donald-trump-steve-bannon-peter-thiel-214490/ |url-status=live }}</ref> A co-founder of [[PayPal]] (1998), [[Palantir Technologies]] (2003), and [[Founders Fund]] (2005), he was also the first outside investor in [[Facebook]] (2004).<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2017/09/donald-trump-peter-thiel-top-intelligence-advisory-post |title=Is Trump Mulling Peter Thiel for a Top Intelligence Advisory Post?|last=Ciralsky |first=Adam |magazine=[[Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]]|date=20 September 2017|language=en|access-date=25 April 2020|archive-date=18 September 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200918150333/https://www.vanityfair.com/news/2017/09/donald-trump-peter-thiel-top-intelligence-advisory-post |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=":2" /> According to ''[[The New York Times]]'', as of December 2025, Thiel's estimated net worth stood at US$27.5 billion, placing him among the 100 richest individuals in the world.<ref name=Forbes>{{Cite magazine|title=Peter Thiel|url=https://www.forbes.com/real-time-billionaires/ |magazine=Forbes|access-date=March 19, 2025}}</ref>

Born in Germany, Thiel moved to the US with his parents when he was an infant. In 1971, his family moved to [[South West Africa|South African-occupied Namibia]],<ref name="McGreal">{{Cite news |last=McGreal |first=Chris |date=26 Jan 2025 |title=How the roots of the 'PayPal mafia' extend to apartheid South Africa |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/jan/26/elon-musk-peter-thiel-apartheid-south-africa? |work=The Guardian}}</ref> before moving back to the US in 1977.<ref name="McGreal" /> After graduating from [[Stanford University]], Thiel worked as a clerk, a [[security (finance)|securities]] lawyer, a speechwriter, and a [[Derivative (finance)|derivatives]] trader at [[Credit Suisse]]. He founded Thiel Capital Management in 1996 and co-founded PayPal with [[Max Levchin]] and [[Luke Nosek]] in 1998. He was PayPal's [[chief executive officer]] until its sale to [[eBay]] in 2002 for $1.5 billion.

Thiel then founded [[Clarium Capital]], a [[global macro]] [[hedge fund]] based in San Francisco.<ref name=insider-2023>{{Cite web |last=Mann |first=Jyoti |title=Peter Thiel paid staff an extra $1,000 a month if they lived close to the office so they were more likely to work late, book says |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/peter-thiel-paid-staff-monthly-bonus-live-near-office-book-2023-7 |access-date=February 14, 2024 |website=[[Business Insider]] |language=en-US |archive-date=14 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240214171827/https://www.businessinsider.com/peter-thiel-paid-staff-monthly-bonus-live-near-office-book-2023-7 |url-status=live }}</ref> He launched Palantir in 2003, a [[big data]] analysis company, and has been its chairman since its inception. In August 2004, Thiel became Facebook's first outside investor by acquiring a 10.2% stake in the company for $500,000. In 2005, he launched Founders Fund with PayPal partners [[Ken Howery]] and Luke Nosek. He co-founded [[Valar Ventures]] in 2010, founded [[Thiel Capital]] in 2011, co-founded [[Mithril Capital]] in 2012, was investment committee chair in 2012, and was a part-time partner at [[Y Combinator (company)|Y Combinator]] from 2015 to 2017.<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 November 2017 |title=Y Combinator Cuts Ties With Peter Thiel After Ending Part-Time Partner Program |url=https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/ryanmac/y-combinator-cuts-ties-with-peter-thiel-ends-part-time |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210814063422/https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/ryanmac/y-combinator-cuts-ties-with-peter-thiel-ends-part-time |archive-date=14 August 2021 |access-date=14 August 2021 |website=BuzzFeed News |language=en}}</ref><ref name="PayPalMafia">{{cite news |title=The PayPal Mafia: Who are they and where are Silicon Valley's richest group of men now? |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/technology/11106473/The-PayPal-Mafia-Who-are-they-and-where-are-Silicon-Valleys-richest-group-of-men-now.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140922203203/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/technology/11106473/The-PayPal-Mafia-Who-are-they-and-where-are-Silicon-Valleys-richest-group-of-men-now.html |archive-date=22 September 2014 |access-date=23 September 2014 |newspaper=[[The Daily Telegraph]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Waters |first=Richard |date=20 December 2013 |title=Lunch with the FT: Peter Thiel |url=https://www.ft.com/content/8537d776-664f-11e3-aa10-00144feabdc0 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161115134808/https://www.ft.com/content/8537d776-664f-11e3-aa10-00144feabdc0 |archive-date=15 November 2016 |access-date=15 November 2016 |website=[[Financial Times]]}}</ref><ref name=":6">{{cite news |last=Hook |first=Leslie |date=11 November 2016 |title=Tech elite discovers it may need Peter Thiel |url=https://www.ft.com/content/c2dcf2be-a850-11e6-8898-79a99e2a4de6 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161115132927/https://www.ft.com/content/c2dcf2be-a850-11e6-8898-79a99e2a4de6 |archive-date=15 November 2016 |access-date=15 November 2016 |website=[[Financial Times]]}}</ref> He was granted [[New Zealand nationality law|New Zealand citizenship]] in 2011, which became controversial when made public in 2017.<ref name="nz2011" />

Thiel has been variously portrayed as a [[conservative libertarian]] and [[Criticism of democracy|democracy-skeptic]] [[authoritarian]]. He is described as "above all, a political theologian operating at the heart of the Silicon Valley" by [[Paolo Benanti]] via ''[[Le Grand Continent]]'',<ref name="Benanti">{{cite news |last1=Malik |first1=Matheo |title=L'hérésie américaine : faut-il brûler Peter Thiel ? |url=https://legrandcontinent.eu/fr/2026/03/14/thiel-heresie-benanti/ |work=Le Grand Continent |date=14 March 2026 |language=fr-FR}}</ref> and the "intellectual architect of Silicon Valley's contemporary ethos" by Mark Howard's 2025 paper.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Howard |first1=Mark |title=Contrarian optionality and negative mimesis: venture capital and the institutional logic of Silicon Valley |journal=Distinktion: Journal of Social Theory |date=2025 |pages=1–18 |doi=10.1080/1600910X.2025.2550269 |url=https://doi.org/10.1080/1600910X.2025.2550269|doi-access=free }}</ref>  Commentators and scholars frequently examine the consistency and nature of his views.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Stollberg-Rilinger |first1=Barbara |title=Stollberg-Rilinger über Aufklärung und KI: Wie wurde der Staat zur 'Maschine'? |url=https://www.faz.net/aktuell/feuilleton/debatten/stollberg-rilinger-ueber-aufklaerung-und-ki-wie-wurde-der-staat-zur-maschine-accg-110730301.html |work=FAZ.NET |date=15 October 2025 |language=de }}</ref>{{refn|group=N|Historian [[Barbara Stollberg-Rilinger]]: "Eine technologisch perfektionierte Welt wäre eine moralfreie und im wörtlichen Sinne verantwortungslose Welt". Translated: "A technologically perfected world would be a world devoid of morality and, in the literal sense, without responsibility."{{sfn|Stollberg-Rilinger|2025}}}}<ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Bullard |first1=Laura |title=The Real Stakes, and Real Story, of Peter Thiel's Antichrist Obsession |url=https://www.wired.com/story/the-real-stakes-real-story-peter-thiels-antichrist-obsession/|magazine=Wired |date=30 September 2025}}</ref><ref name="lemonde1"/><ref name="libero">{{cite news |last1=Socci |first1=Antonio |title=Peter Thiel, il Vaticano non demonizza il re dell'Intelligenza artificiale {{!}} Libero Quotidiano.it |url=https://www.liberoquotidiano.it/news/italia/46884259/peter-thiel-vaticano-non-demonizza-re-intelligenza-artificiale/ |work=Libero Quotidiano |language=it}}</ref>

Thiel has made substantial donations to [[Right-wing politics#United states|American right-wing]] figures and causes. Through the [[Thiel Foundation]], Thiel governs the grant-making bodies [[Breakout Labs]] and [[Thiel Fellowship]]. In 2016, when ''[[Gawker]]'' lost in ''[[Bollea v. Gawker]]'', Thiel confirmed that he had funded [[Hulk Hogan|Bollea (Hulk Hogan)]]. ''Gawker'' had previously [[Outing|outed]] Thiel as [[gay]].<ref name="Gawker" />

[[Epstein files|Emails related to the activities]] of convicted child sex offender [[Jeffrey Epstein]] released by the [[United States House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform|House Oversight Committee]] in 2026 revealed that Valar Ventures accepted $40 million from Epstein and that Thiel corresponded with Epstein for five years before [[Death of Jeffrey Epstein|Epstein's death]], including on the topic of [[Brexit]].<ref>{{cite web|title= They Said They Weren't Close to Epstein. New Documents Show Otherwise.|url= https://www.nytimes.com/2026/01/31/us/epstein-powerful-men.html |website=The New York Times|first=Nicholas|last=Confessore|date=31 January 2026|access-date=5 February 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title= Epstein celebrated Brexit and 'return to tribalism', newly released emails suggest|url= https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/politics/jeffrey-epstein-brexit-peter-thiel-b2912853.html |website=The Independent|first=Millie|last=Cook|date=3 February 2026|access-date=5 February 2026}}</ref>

== Early life and education ==
Peter Andreas Thiel was born in [[Frankfurt am Main]], Hesse on 11 October 1967, to Klaus Friedrich Thiel and Susanne Thiel.<ref>{{Cite web |last=O'Dea |first=Meghan |title=Peter Thiel |url=https://www.immigrantentrepreneurship.org/entries/peter-thiel/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181204195127/https://www.immigrantentrepreneurship.org/entry.php?rec=246 |access-date=30 April 2023 |publisher=Immigrant Entrepreneurship |archive-date=4 December 2018}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url=https://www.dia.govt.nz/diawebsite.nsf/Files/Peter-Thiel-release-1-February-2017/$file/Peter-Thiel-release-1-February-2017.pdf|title=Peter Thiel's application for New Zealand citizenship|publisher=New Zealand Department of Internal Affairs|page=65|archive-date=18 February 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190218171435/https://www.dia.govt.nz/diawebsite.nsf/Files/Peter-Thiel-release-1-February-2017/$file/Peter-Thiel-release-1-February-2017.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref> The family migrated to the United States when Peter was one year old and lived in [[Cleveland]], Ohio, where his father worked as a [[chemical engineer]].<ref name=":4" /> Klaus worked for various mining companies, which created an itinerant upbringing for Thiel and his younger brother, Patrick Michael Thiel.<ref name=bloomberg>{{cite news |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2011-02-03/peter-thiel-21st-century-free-radical|first=Romesh|last=Ratnesar|title=Peter Thiel: 21st Century Free Radical|work=[[Bloomberg BusinessWeek]]|date=3 February 2011|url-access=subscription|access-date=13 December 2025|archive-date=5 February 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110205013020/http://www.businessweek.com//print//magazine//content//11_07//b4215072350752.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> Thiel and his mother later naturalized as U.S. citizens, whereas his father did not.<ref name=":0" /><ref name="DW" />

Before settling in [[Foster City, California]], in 1977, the Thiel family lived in [[South Africa]] and [[South West Africa]] (modern-day [[Namibia]]) in the time of [[apartheid]]. Peter changed elementary schools seven times. He attended a German-language school in [[Swakopmund]] for two years that required students to wear uniforms and employed [[corporal punishment]], such as striking students' hands with a ruler. He said this experience instilled a distaste for uniformity and regimentation later reflected in his support for [[individualism]] and [[libertarianism]].<ref>{{harvnb|Packer|2013|pages=120–121}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052748704696304575537882643165738 |newspaper=The Wall Street Journal|title=Technology=Salvation|last=Jenkins|first=Holman W. Jr.|date=9 October 2010|access-date=13 December 2025|archive-date=26 September 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140926025107/http://online.wsj.com/news/articles/SB10001424052748704696304575537882643165738|url-status=live|url-access=subscription}}</ref> The German community in Swakopmund was known at the time for its continued glorification of [[Nazism]].<ref name="McGreal" /> Thiel was noted as a smart, but lonely, withdrawn boy, with whom others would not mingle because they knew he would not stay long in the town.{{sfn|Chafkin|2021|p=5}}

Thiel played ''[[Dungeons & Dragons]]'' and was an avid reader of [[science fiction]], with [[Isaac Asimov]] and [[Robert A. Heinlein]] among his favorite authors. He is a fan of [[J. R. R. Tolkien]]'s works, stating as an adult that he had read ''[[The Lord of the Rings]]'' more than 10 times.<ref name=Brown2014>{{cite news|last1=Brown|first1=Mick|title=Peter Thiel: the billionaire tech entrepreneur on a mission to cheat death|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/technology/11098971/Peter-Thiel-the-billionaire-tech-entrepreneur-on-a-mission-to-cheat-death.html|access-date=16 October 2014|newspaper=[[The Daily Telegraph]]|date=19 September 2014|archive-date=18 October 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141018164619/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/technology/11098971/Peter-Thiel-the-billionaire-tech-entrepreneur-on-a-mission-to-cheat-death.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Thiel excelled in [[mathematics]] and scored first in a California-wide mathematics competition while attending [[Bowditch Middle School]] in [[Foster City]].<ref name="fortune" /> At [[San Mateo High School]], he read [[Ayn Rand]] and, influenced by his parents, admired [[Richard Nixon|Nixon]] and [[Ronald Reagan|Reagan]].{{sfn|Chafkin|2021|p=30}} He was [[valedictorian]] of his graduating class in 1985.<ref name="fortune" /><ref>{{cite web|url=http://abc7news.com/technology/only-on-abc7-billionaire-weighs-in-on-silicon-valleys-future/320291/|first=Ama|last=Daetz|title=Billionaire weighs in on Silicon Valley's future|publisher=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]]|date=22 September 2014|access-date=18 November 2016|archive-date=19 November 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161119064337/http://abc7news.com/technology/only-on-abc7-billionaire-weighs-in-on-silicon-valleys-future/320291/|url-status=live}}</ref> When at school, he reportedly charged $500 each to take the SAT for [[underclassman (student)|underclassmen]], knowing this would cost him his spot at Stanford if discovered.{{sfn|Chafkin|2021|p=30}}

Thiel studied [[philosophy]] at [[Stanford University]]. The replacement of a "[[Western Culture]]" program at Stanford with a "Culture, Ideas and Values" course that addressed diversity and [[multiculturalism]] prompted Thiel to co-found ''[[The Stanford Review]]'', a [[conservatism|conservative]] and [[libertarianism|libertarian]] newspaper, in 1987. The paper received funding from [[Irving Kristol]].<ref>{{harvnb|Packer|2013|page=125}}</ref> Thiel was ''The Stanford Review's'' first [[editor-in-chief]] until he graduated in 1989. Thiel has maintained his relationship with the paper, consulting with staff and donating to the newspaper.<ref name="Granato">{{cite web |last1=Granato |first1=Andrew |title=Stanford Politics |url=https://stanfordpolitics.org/2017/11/27/peter-thiel-cover-story/ |website=stanfordpolitics.org |date=27 November 2017 |access-date=26 February 2021 |archive-date=7 February 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210207104449/https://stanfordpolitics.org/2017/11/27/peter-thiel-cover-story/ |url-status=live }}</ref> According to [[Marc Andreessen]], Thiel's time at the ''Review'' marked the beginning of a career-long strategy: using provocative verbal cues{{snd}}later delivered through talks, books and mottos{{snd}}as a way to attract capable individuals to his projects, preferring this method over actively seeking out talent. The ''Review'' has spawned a large network of industry leaders, among whom Andrew Granato and the ''[[Fortune (magazine)|Fortune]]'' respectively identify around 300 people who have worked for or received investment from Peter Thiel or [[Joe Lonsdale]], another prominent editor-in-chief and Thiel's mentee. A number of ''Review'' alumni have also become public officials, beginning with [[Jay Bhattacharya]] and Paula M. Stannard who were editors during Thiel's time as editor-in-chief.<ref name="Granato"/><ref>{{cite news |last1=Mathews |first1=Jessica |title=Peter Thiel launched a student newspaper 36 years ago. It has quietly become one of the surest paths to success in Silicon Valley |url=https://fortune.com/2023/08/24/peter-thiel-student-newspaper-stanford-review/ |work=Fortune |date=24 August 2023 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20260226154206/https://fortune.com/2023/08/24/peter-thiel-student-newspaper-stanford-review/ |archive-date=26 February 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last1=Lalka |first1=Rob |title=The Venture Alchemists: How Big Tech Turned Profits Into Power |date=14 May 2024 |publisher=Columbia University Press |isbn=978-0-231-55833-4 |page=42 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=YdvtEAAAQBAJ&pg=PP42 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Marc Andreessen on Learning to Love the Humanities (Ep. 152) |url=https://conversationswithtyler.com/episodes/marc-andreessen/ |access-date=7 October 2025 |date=7 July 2018}}</ref>

After graduation, Thiel attended [[Stanford Law School]], graduating in 1992 with a [[Juris Doctor]] degree.<ref name="Paypal,-Inc-Jan-2002-S-1/A">{{cite web |url=http://edgar.secdatabase.com/2504/91205702003491/filing-main.htm |title=Paypal, Inc, Form S-1/A, Filing Date Jan 31, 2002 |publisher=[[Electronic Data Gathering, Analysis, and Retrieval]] |access-date=14 May 2018 |archive-date=15 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180515112212/http://edgar.secdatabase.com/2504/91205702003491/filing-main.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> While at Stanford, Thiel met [[René Girard]], whose [[mimetic theory]] influenced him.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://businessinsider.com/peter-thiel-on-rene-girards-influence-2014-11|first=Richard|last=Feloni|title=Peter Thiel explains how an esoteric philosophy book shaped his worldview|website=[[Business Insider]]|date=10 November 2014|access-date=22 November 2016|archive-date=22 November 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161122220837/http://www.businessinsider.com/peter-thiel-on-rene-girards-influence-2014-11|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|first=René|last=Girard|title=Things Hidden Since the Foundation of the World|publisher=Stanford University Press|date=1987|pages=7, 26, 307}}</ref>

== Career ==
=== Early career ===
After graduating from Stanford Law School, Thiel was a [[law clerk]] to Judge [[James Larry Edmondson]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit|U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit]] from 1992 to 1993.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://fortune.com/2016/09/15/trump-thiel-supreme-court/|title=Rumor About Trump's Supreme Court Pick Causes a Stir But Appears Untrue|first=Jeff John|last=Roberts|website=Fortune|date=15 September 2016|access-date=22 May 2017|archive-date=1 January 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170101071209/http://fortune.com/2016/09/15/trump-thiel-supreme-court/|url-status=live}}</ref> Thiel then worked as a [[Security (finance)|securities]] lawyer for [[Sullivan & Cromwell]] in New York. He left the law firm in under a year.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://stanfordlawyer.law.stanford.edu/2011/05/q-a-legal-matters-with-peter-thiel-%E2%80%9992-ba-%E2%80%9989-bs-%E2%80%9989-and-mark-a-lemley-ba-%E2%80%9988/|title=Articles Archive|publisher=Stanford Law School|access-date=29 May 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305191909/https://stanfordlawyer.law.stanford.edu/2011/05/q-a-legal-matters-with-peter-thiel-%E2%80%9992-ba-%E2%80%9989-bs-%E2%80%9989-and-mark-a-lemley-ba-%E2%80%9988/|archive-date=5 March 2016|url-status=dead}}</ref> He then took a job as a [[Derivative (finance)|derivatives]] trader in currency options at [[Credit Suisse]] in 1993 while also working as a speechwriter for former [[United States Secretary of Education|United States secretary of education]] [[William Bennett]]. Thiel returned to California in 1996.<ref name="BW01">{{Cite news |date=2004-11-08 |title=Big Bucks From Bubble Fears |language=en |work=Bloomberg.com |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2004-11-07/big-bucks-from-bubble-fears |access-date=2023-04-30 |archive-date=7 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210507205752/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2004-11-07/big-bucks-from-bubble-fears |url-status=live }}</ref>

Upon returning to the [[San Francisco Bay Area|Bay Area]], Thiel capitalized on the [[dot-com boom]]. With financial support from friends and family, he raised $1 million toward the establishment of Thiel Capital Management and embarked on his venture capital career. Early on, he experienced a setback after investing $100,000 in his friend [[Luke Nosek]]'s unsuccessful web-based calendar project. Soon thereafter, Nosek's friend [[Max Levchin]] described to Thiel his [[cryptography]]-related company idea, which became their first venture called Fieldlink (later renamed [[Confinity]]) in 1998.<ref name="thiel disagrees">{{cite news |last1=Parloff |first1=Roger |title=Peter Thiel disagrees with you |url=https://fortune.com/2014/09/04/peter-thiels-contrarian-strategy/ |work=Fortune |date=4 September 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230215031624/https://fortune.com/2014/09/04/peter-thiels-contrarian-strategy/ |archive-date=15 February 2023 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.ft.com/content/86432398-4897-11e4-9d04-00144feab7de|title=Timeline: The rise of PayPal|first=Mark|last=Odell|website=Financial Times|date=30 September 2014|access-date=10 October 2023|archive-date=22 June 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200622163106/https://www.ft.com/content/86432398-4897-11e4-9d04-00144feab7de|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== PayPal ===
{{further|PayPal#Early history}}

Thiel provided the initial $100,000 for Fieldlink in 1998.{{sfn|Soni|2022|p=29}} In February 1999, they raised $500,000 largely from friends and family ($35,000 was from Thiel's parents).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Soni |first1=Jimmy |title=The Founders: The Story of Paypal and the Entrepreneurs Who Shaped Silicon Valley |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=0DZbEAAAQBAJ&pg=PA70 |publisher=Simon and Schuster |date=22 February 2022 |page=70 |isbn=978-1-5011-9726-0 |language=en}}</ref> By middle 1999, they raised $4.5 million, with [[Nokia#NGP Capital|Nokia Ventures]] contributing $3 million.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Nicholas |first1=Tom |title=VC: An American History |date=9 July 2019 |publisher=Harvard University Press |isbn=978-0-674-98800-2 |page=284 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=uSmWDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA284 |language=en}}</ref>

With Confinity, Thiel realized they could develop software to bridge a gap in making online payments. Although the use of [[credit card]]s and expanding [[Cash machine|automated teller machine]] networks provided consumers with more payment options, not all merchants had the necessary hardware to accept credit cards. Thus, consumers had to pay with exact cash or check. Thiel wanted to create a type of digital wallet for consumer convenience and security by encrypting data on digital devices, and in 1999 Confinity launched [[PayPal]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://vator.tv/news/2018-02-05-when-paypal-was-young-the-early-years|title=When PayPal was young: the early years|date=5 February 2018|website=VatorNews|access-date=30 December 2019|archive-date=30 December 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191230135116/https://vator.tv/news/2018-02-05-when-paypal-was-young-the-early-years|url-status=live}}</ref>

PayPal promised to open up new possibilities for handling money. Thiel viewed PayPal's mission as liberating people from the erosion of the value of their currencies due to inflation.{{sfn|Jackson|2004|p=}}{{refn|group=N|Peter Thiel: "Everyone in the world needs money – to get paid, to trade, to live. Paper money is an ancient technology and an inconvenient means of payment. You can run out of it. It wears out. It can get lost or stolen. In the twenty-first century, people need a form of money that's more convenient and secure, something that can be accessed from anywhere with a PDA or an Internet connection. Of course, what we're calling 'convenient' for American users will be revolutionary for the developing world. Many of these countries' governments play fast and loose with their currencies. They use inflation and sometimes wholesale currency devaluations, like we saw in Russia and several Southeast Asian countries last year, to take wealth away from their citizens. Most of the ordinary people there never have an opportunity to open an offshore account or to get their hands on more than a few bills of a stable currency like U.S. dollars. Eventually PayPal will be able to change this. In the future, when we make our service available outside the U.S. and as Internet penetration continues to expand to all economic tiers of people, PayPal will give citizens worldwide more direct control over their currencies than they ever had before. It will be nearly impossible for corrupt governments to steal wealth from their people through their old means because if they try the people will switch to dollars or Pounds or Yen, in effect dumping the worthless local currency for something more secure."{{sfn|Jackson|2004|pp=321–327}}}}

When PayPal launched at a press conference in 1999, representatives from [[Nokia]] and [[Deutsche Bank]] sent $3 million in venture funding to Thiel using PayPal on their [[PalmPilot]]s. PayPal then continued to grow through mergers in 2000 with [[Elon Musk]]'s online financial services company [[X.com (bank)|X.com]], and with [[Pixo]], a company specializing in mobile commerce. These mergers allowed PayPal to expand into the wireless phone market and transformed it into a safer and more user-friendly tool by enabling users to transfer money via a free online registration and email rather than by exchanging bank account information. PayPal [[Initial public offering|went public]] on 15 February 2002 and was bought by [[eBay]] for $1.5 billion in October of that year.<ref>{{cite web |title=eBay picks up PayPal for $1.5 billion by Margaret Kane |url=http://www.news.com/2100-1017-941964.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://www.webcitation.org/5ZJ3x0dGI?url=http://news.cnet.com/2100-1017-941964.html?hhTest=1 |archive-date= 2008-07-14|access-date=24 January 2008 |website=[[CNET News]] |quote=On Monday, eBay said it is acquiring online payments company PayPal in a deal valued at $1.5 billion.}}</ref> Thiel remained CEO of the company until the sale.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|url=https://www.latimes.com/business/technology/la-fi-tn-who-is-peter-thiel-20180215-story.html|title=Peter Thiel's resume includes PayPal, Facebook and supporting Trump. And he's coming to L.A.|last=Lien|first=Tracey|website=[[Los Angeles Times]]|date=15 February 2018|access-date=24 July 2018|archive-date=24 July 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180724213615/http://www.latimes.com/business/technology/la-fi-tn-who-is-peter-thiel-20180215-story.html|url-status=live}}</ref> His 3.7% stake in the company was worth $55 million at the time of acquisition.<ref>{{cite web|title=SEC Info – Ebay Inc – S-4 – on 8/6/02|access-date=24 January 2008|url=http://www.secinfo.com/dr6nd.33fd.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061214082119/http://www.secinfo.com/dr6nd.33fd.htm|url-status=live|archive-date=14 December 2006}}</ref> In Silicon Valley circles, Thiel is colloquially referred to as the "Don of the [[PayPal Mafia]]".<ref>{{Cite news|title=The PayPal mafia – November 14, 2007|access-date=24 January 2008|url=https://money.cnn.com/2007/11/13/magazines/fortune/paypal_mafia.fortune/index.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080720140543/http://money.cnn.com/2007/11/13/magazines/fortune/paypal_mafia.fortune/index.htm|archive-date=20 July 2008|work=CNN|date=14 November 2007|first=Jeffrey M.|last=O'Brien|url-status=dead}}</ref>

=== Clarium Capital ===
{{further|Clarium Capital}}
Thiel used $10 million of his proceeds to create [[Clarium Capital Management]], a [[global macro]] [[hedge fund]] focusing on directional and liquid instruments in currencies, interest rates, commodities, and equities.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.britannica.com/biography/Peter-Thiel|title=Peter Thiel {{!}} Biography & Facts|website=Encyclopedia Britannica|language=en|access-date=19 March 2020|archive-date=16 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200416101821/https://www.britannica.com/biography/Peter-Thiel|url-status=live}}</ref> Thiel stated that "the big, macroeconomic idea that we had at Clarium—the [[Idée fixe (psychology)|idée fixe]]—was the [[Hubbert peak theory|peak-oil theory]], which was basically that the world was running out of oil, and that there were no easy alternatives."<ref name=fortune/>

In 2003, Thiel successfully bet that the United States dollar would weaken.<ref name=":1">{{Cite news|url=https://www.businessinsider.com/peter-thiel-facebook-trump-biography-2018-2#when-paypal-was-sold-in-2002-thiels-stake-was-worth-about-55-million-which-he-used-to-launch-a-hedge-fund-clarium-capital-by-the-time-he-was-37-clarium-was-managing-270-million-8|title=The life and rise of billionaire investor Peter Thiel, Trump's biggest Silicon Valley supporter—who might be moving to Los Angeles|work=Business Insider|access-date=17 August 2018|archive-date=18 August 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180818020826/https://www.businessinsider.com/peter-thiel-facebook-trump-biography-2018-2#when-paypal-was-sold-in-2002-thiels-stake-was-worth-about-55-million-which-he-used-to-launch-a-hedge-fund-clarium-capital-by-the-time-he-was-37-clarium-was-managing-270-million-8|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2004, Thiel spoke of the [[dot-com bubble]] having migrated, in effect, into a growing bubble in the financial sector, and specified [[General Electric]] and [[Walmart]] as vulnerable. In 2005, Clarium saw a 57.1% return as Thiel predicted that the dollar would rally.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB113961793285971352|title=A Second Payday for PayPal Founder|last=Savitz|first=Eric J.|date=13 February 2006|work=The Wall Street Journal|access-date=17 August 2018|language=en-US|issn=0099-9660|archive-date=18 August 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180818020854/https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB113961793285971352|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=":1" />

However, Clarium faltered in 2006 with a 7.8% loss. Thereafter, the firm sought to profit in the long-term from its petrodollar analysis, which foresaw the impending decline in oil supplies.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.ft.com/content/0324f82c-fcc5-11db-9971-000b5df10621|title=Clarium Capital bets on rates to rise more|date=7 May 2007|access-date=10 October 2023|first=James|last=Mackintosh|publisher=Financial Times|archive-date=23 October 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023144351/https://www.ft.com/content/0324f82c-fcc5-11db-9971-000b5df10621|url-status=live}}</ref> Clarium's [[assets under management]] grew after achieving a 40.3% return in 2007 to more than $7 billion by the first quarter of 2008, but fell later in the year and again in 2009 after financial markets collapsed.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/clarium-returns/hedge-fund-clarium-takes-9-9-pct-hit-in-march-note-idUKN0434980720080404|title=Hedge fund Clarium takes 9.9 pct hit in March-note|date=4 April 2008|access-date=10 October 2023|first=Dane|last=Hamilton|publisher=Reuters|archive-date=23 October 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023144351/https://www.reuters.com/article/clarium-returns/hedge-fund-clarium-takes-9-9-pct-hit-in-march-note-idUKN0434980720080404|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.businessinsider.com/peter-thiels-hedge-fund-lost-tons-of-money-this-year-2010-1|title=Peter Thiel: Down 25% In 2009, And 10% In December Alone|date=11 January 2010|access-date=10 October 2023|first=Courtney|last=Comstock|publisher=Business Insider|archive-date=23 October 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023144356/https://www.businessinsider.com/peter-thiels-hedge-fund-lost-tons-of-money-this-year-2010-1|url-status=live}}</ref> By 2011, after missing out on the economic rebound, many key investors pulled out, reducing the value of Clarium's assets to $350 million, two thirds of which was Thiel's money.<ref name=newyorker>{{cite magazine|title=No Death, No Taxes|access-date=13 December 2025|first=George|last=Packer|magazine=[[The New Yorker]]|url=https://www.newyorker.com/reporting/2011/11/28/111128fa_fact_packer?currentpage=all|date=28 November 2011|archive-date=5 January 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120105040836/http://www.newyorker.com/reporting/2011/11/28/111128fa_fact_packer?currentPage=all|url-status=dead|url-access=limited}}</ref>

=== Palantir ===
{{further|Palantir Technologies}}

Palantir Technologies, a [[big data]] analysis company named after the [[Palantír|Tolkien artifact,]] was formed as a reaction to the [[September 11 attacks]], largely building on PayPal's anti-fraud technology.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Lee |first1=Newton |title=Counterterrorism and Cybersecurity: Total Information Awareness |date=7 April 2015 |publisher=Springer |isbn=978-3-319-17244-6 |pages=157,158 |url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Counterterrorism_and_Cybersecurity/zSv3BwAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=thiel+%22built-in%22+Palantir&pg=PA157 |language=en}}</ref> Thiel stated that the attacks opened the way for all sorts of draconian policies, so the best method would be providing cutting-edge technology with built-in policing systems to ensure the state use its power legally. Analyst [[Christopher Soghoian]] later commented that Palantir does provide these policing systems, but the state can turn them off.{{sfn|Lee|2015|pp=157,158}}<ref>{{Cite web|date=22 November 2011|title=Palantir, the War on Terror's Secret Weapon|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2011-11-22/palantir-the-war-on-terrors-secret-weapon|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200922010507/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2011-11-22/palantir-the-war-on-terrors-secret-weapon|archive-date=22 September 2020|access-date=26 September 2020|website=Bloomberg.com}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/andygreenberg/2013/08/14/agent-of-intelligence-how-a-deviant-philosopher-built-palantir-a-cia-funded-data-mining-juggernaut/2/|title=How A 'Deviant' Philosopher Built Palantir, A CIA-Funded Data-Mining Juggernaut|author=Andy Greenberg|work=Forbes|access-date=7 June 2015|archive-date=27 June 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150627001145/http://www.forbes.com/sites/andygreenberg/2013/08/14/agent-of-intelligence-how-a-deviant-philosopher-built-palantir-a-cia-funded-data-mining-juggernaut/2/|url-status=live}}</ref>

In May 2003, Thiel incorporated Palantir.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://qz.com/1346926/the-hidden-logic-of-peter-thiels-lord-of-the-rings-inspired-company-names/|title=The hidden logic of Peter Thiel's "Lord of the Rings"-inspired company names|last=Wexner|first=David|website=Quartz|date=4 August 2018 |language=en|access-date=19 March 2020|archive-date=19 March 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200319182534/https://qz.com/1346926/the-hidden-logic-of-peter-thiels-lord-of-the-rings-inspired-company-names/|url-status=live}}</ref> He serves as chairman of the company.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Okumura |first1=Shigesaburo |title=Japan should think twice before entrusting Palantir with sensitive data |url=https://asia.nikkei.com/spotlight/comment/japan-should-think-twice-before-entrusting-palantir-with-sensitive-data |work=Nikkei Asia |date=5 June 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

Although the software only analyzes data collected by the customers, some criticize it for enabling surveillance.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hvistendahl |first=Mara |date=January 30, 2021 |title=How the LAPD and Palantir Use Data to Justify Racist Policing |url=https://theintercept.com/2021/01/30/lapd-palantir-data-driven-policing/ |access-date=June 24, 2025 |website=The Intercept |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Haskins |first1=Caroline |title=What Does Palantir Actually Do? |url=https://www.wired.com/story/palantir-what-the-company-does/ |url-status=live |date=11 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250907131548/https://www.wired.com/story/palantir-what-the-company-does/ |archive-date=7 September 2025}}</ref>{{refn|group=N|''Wired'': "Palantir is often called a data broker, a data miner, or a giant database of personal information. In reality, it's none of these [...] Customers need to already have the data they want to work with—Palantir itself does not provide any."{{sfn|Haskins|2025}}}}<ref>{{Britannica URL|url=https://www.britannica.com/money/Palantir-Technologies-Inc|title=Palantir Technologies, Inc.|author=Karl Montevirgen}}</ref> Professor Vasilis Galis notes that the software is not a mere tool, but shapes policing culture with new policing norms.{{sfn|Galis|Karlsson|2024|pp=2438–2456}}{{refn|group=N|Vasilis Galis, Professor in science and technology studies at the [[IT University of Copenhagen]]: "When Palantir Technologies customized the Gotham platform into POL-INTEL, a data integration and analysis platform purchased and used by the Danish police, it also enforced a new ontology that simultaneously shaped the police organization and policing practices. [...] The basis for Palantir's E-ontology derives from such political notions and is explicitly performed by the company's own under-standing of its products: 'software as much as anything else is a product of the legal and moral order from which it stems and plays a role in defending it' (Palantir,2022a)."{{sfn|Galis|Karlsson|2024|pp=2438–2456}}}}

According to Geoff Shullenberger (managing director of ''[[Compact (American magazine)|Compact]]'') and Moira Weigel (Assistant Professor of Comparative Literature, Harvard), Peter Thiel and Alex Karp built Palantir on the basis of their understanding of [[Leo Strauss]] and the [[Frankfurt School]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Shullenberger |first1=Geoff |title=The Intellectual Origins of Surveillance Tech |url=https://outsidertheory.com/the-intellectual-origins-of-surveillance-tech/ |website=OUTSIDER THEORY |date=17 July 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Moira Weigel — Palantir Goes to the Frankfurt School |url=https://www.boundary2.org/2020/07/moira-weigel-palantir-goes-to-the-frankfurt-school/#authorbio |website=b2o: boundary 2 online |date=10 July 2020}}</ref>

=== Facebook ===
{{further|Facebook#Thefacebook, Thiel investment, and name change}}

After the first meeting with Zuckerberg in August 2004, Thiel made a $500,000 [[Angel investor|angel investment]] in the startup (then "a three-person dormroom") for a 10.2% stake in the company and joined Facebook's board. This was the first outside investment in Facebook and valued the company at $4.9 million. He also bought Zuckerberg a car.<ref name="lifeafterFacebook"/><ref>{{cite news |last1=Cao |first1=Sissi |title=Facebook's First Investor Peter Thiel Owns Less Than 10K Shares After Latest Stock Dump |url=https://observer.com/2020/02/peter-thiel-dump-facebook-stock-before-2020-election/ |work=Observer |date=11 February 2020}}</ref> The investment was originally in the form of a [[convertible note]], to be converted to equity if Facebook reached 1.5 million users by the end of 2004. Although Facebook narrowly missed the target, Thiel allowed the loan to be converted to equity anyway.<ref name="kirkpatrick-thiel">{{cite book |last=Kirkpatrick |first=David |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=PxTvbM-VCPEC |title=The Facebook Effect |date=2010 |publisher=Simon & Schuster |isbn=978-1-4391-0212-1 |pages=87–88}}</ref> Thiel said of his investment: "I was comfortable with them pursuing their original vision. And it was a very reasonable valuation. I thought it was going to be a pretty safe investment."<ref name="kirkpatrick-thiel" /><ref name="G01">{{Cite web|url=http://www.theguardian.com/technology/2010/aug/25/facebook-value-flotation|title=Shareholder trading values Facebook at more than $33bn|date=25 August 2010|website=The Guardian|access-date=17 May 2020|archive-date=14 June 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200614164029/https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2010/aug/25/facebook-value-flotation|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="lifeafterFacebook">{{cite news |title=Life After Facebook |first1=Brian |last1=Caulfield |first2=Nicole |last2=Perlroth |url=https://www.forbes.com/forbes/2011/0214/features-peter-thiel-social-media-life-after-facebook_3.html |work=[[Forbes]] |date=14 February 2011 |access-date=29 November 2011 |archive-date=23 March 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130323181425/http://www.forbes.com/forbes/2011/0214/features-peter-thiel-social-media-life-after-facebook_3.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Facebook employees reported that Thiel's influence was unusual for a board member who was not also the CEO. Some criticized Thiel for pushing his mentee Zuckerberg and the company to the right.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cucci |first1=Dorothy |title=Peter Thiel was the 'puppet master' behind Facebook's political dealings, and 6 other unusual details about the billionaire tycoon's relationship with Mark Zuckerberg |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/mark-zuckerberg-peter-thiel-strange-relationship-contrarian-max-chafkin-2021-9 |work=Business Insider |date=8 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220430050803/https://www.businessinsider.com/mark-zuckerberg-peter-thiel-strange-relationship-contrarian-max-chafkin-2021-9 |url-status=live |archive-date=30 April 2022}}</ref> Zuckerberg credited Thiel with helping him time Facebook's 2007 Series D, which closed before the [[2008 financial crisis]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Kirkpatrick |first=David |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=PxTvbM-VCPEC |title=The Facebook Effect: The Inside Story of the Company That Is Connecting the World |date=8 June 2010 |publisher=Simon and Schuster |isbn=978-1-4391-0212-1 |edition= |pages=246 |language=en}}</ref>

[[Initial public offering of Facebook|Facebook's initial public offering]] was in May 2012, with a market cap of nearly $100 billion ($38 a share), at which time Thiel sold 16.8 million shares for $638 million.<ref name="betabeat">{{Cite web |date=2013-01-30 |title=Peter Thiel Sells Almost All of His Facebook Stock, Donates $1 Million to a Tea Party PAC {{!}} Betabeat |url=http://betabeat.com/2012/08/peter-thiel-sells-almost-all-facebook-stock-tea-party-donation-08202012/ |access-date=2023-04-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130130225527/http://betabeat.com/2012/08/peter-thiel-sells-almost-all-facebook-stock-tea-party-donation-08202012/ |archive-date=30 January 2013 }}</ref> In August 2012, immediately upon the conclusion of the early investor lock-up period, Thiel sold almost all of his remaining stake for between $19.27 and $20.69 per share, or $395.8 million, for a total of more than $1 billion.<ref name="2012fb">{{cite news |author=Oreskovic |first=Alexei |date=19 August 2012 |title=Facebook director Thiel sold 20 million shares after lock-up |work=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-facebook-thiel-idUSBRE87J0UQ20120820 |url-status=live |access-date=19 August 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160817131143/http://www.reuters.com/article/us-facebook-thiel-idUSBRE87J0UQ20120820 |archive-date=17 August 2016}}</ref> In 2016, he sold a little under 1 million of his shares for around $100 million. In November 2017, he sold another 160,805 shares for $29 million, putting his holdings in Facebook at 59,913 Class A shares.<ref>{{cite news |date=22 November 2017 |title=Peter Thiel sells most of remaining Facebook stake |work=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-facebook-stake/peter-thiel-sells-most-of-remaining-facebook-stake-idUSKBN1DM2BQ |url-status=live |access-date=24 November 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171124212128/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-facebook-stake/peter-thiel-sells-most-of-remaining-facebook-stake-idUSKBN1DM2BQ |archive-date=24 November 2017}}</ref> As of April 2020, he owned less than 10,000 shares in Facebook.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2017/11/22/peter-thiel-sells-majority-of-facebook-shares-but-2012-was-bigger.html|title=Peter Thiel just sold more Facebook shares, but his big mistake was dumping them in 2012|last=Shinal|first=John|date=22 November 2017|website=CNBC|language=en|access-date=14 April 2020|archive-date=16 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200416101824/https://www.cnbc.com/2017/11/22/peter-thiel-sells-majority-of-facebook-shares-but-2012-was-bigger.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 7 February 2022, Thiel announced he would not stand for re-election to the board of Facebook owner Meta at the 2022 annual stockholders' meeting and would leave after 17 years in order to support pro–[[Donald Trump]] candidates in the [[2022 United States elections]].<ref name=chair>{{cite web |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2022/02/07/peter-thiel-to-step-down-from-facebook-board.html |title=Peter Thiel to step down from board of Facebook parent Meta |work=[[CNBC]] |date=7 February 2022 |first1=Lauren |last1=Feiner |first2=Ari |last2=Levy |access-date=7 February 2022 |archive-date=7 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220207211354/https://www.cnbc.com/2022/02/07/peter-thiel-to-step-down-from-facebook-board.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Peter Thiel to leave Meta board to pursue Trump political agenda |url=https://www.jpost.com/business-and-innovation/article-695874 |access-date=9 February 2022 |website=[[The Jerusalem Post]] |date=8 February 2022 |language=en-US |archive-date=8 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220208213606/https://www.jpost.com/business-and-innovation/article-695874 |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Founders Fund ===
{{further|Founders Fund}}
In 2005, Thiel created Founders Fund, a San Francisco-based venture capital fund.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://block.one/news/block-one-closes-strategic-investment-round-led-by-peter-thiel-and-bitmain/|title=Block.one Closes Strategic Investment Round Led by Peter Thiel and Bitmain|access-date=1 November 2018|archive-date=16 September 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180916153057/https://block.one/news/block-one-closes-strategic-investment-round-led-by-peter-thiel-and-bitmain/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=insider-2023 /> Other partners in the fund include [[Sean Parker]], [[Ken Howery]], and [[Luke Nosek]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.entrepreneur.com/article/249705|title=What Types of Companies Is Peter Thiel's Founders Fund Actually Investing In?|last=Siu|first=Eric|date=25 August 2015|work=Entrepreneur|access-date=11 April 2018|language=en|archive-date=12 April 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180412082159/https://www.entrepreneur.com/article/249705|url-status=live}}</ref>

The fund focuses on defense-related startups and technology.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bort |first1=Julie |title=Founders Fund's new $4.6B fund indicates an era of big VC spending |url=https://techcrunch.com/2025/04/15/founders-funds-new-4-6b-fund-indicates-an-era-of-big-vc-spending/ |work=TechCrunch |date=15 April 2025}}</ref> After Facebook, even though it was a big financial success, Thiel made the Founders Fund pivot to hard tech, with the reasoning that while companies like Twitter might have a high value, they would not take "civilization to the next level."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wachtel |first1=Katya |title=Peter Thiel Explains Why He'd Never Buy Twitter Shares, And What He's Interested In Instead|url=https://www.businessinsider.com/peter-thiel-tech-stocks-anti-twitter-clarium-returns-2011-5 |access-date=26 July 2025 |work=Business Insider |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110523053048/https://www.businessinsider.com/peter-thiel-tech-stocks-anti-twitter-clarium-returns-2011-5 |url-status=live |archive-date=23 May 2011 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Gabriele |first1=Mario |title=How Did SpaceX, Facebook, and Founders Fund Conquer the VC Industry with a Contrarian Philosophy? |url=https://www.itiger.com/news/2553476677 |access-date=26 July 2025 |work=Tiger Brokers |language=en |archive-date=2 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250802231727/https://www.itiger.com/news/2553476677 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The ''Economist'' notes that the fund and Thiel, personally, have a history of incubating startups that do hypersensitive work related to national security. The fund casts [[Palantir Technologies|Palantir]], [[Anduril Industries|Anduril]] and the newly minted nuclear startup General Matter as the three parts of a trilogy, to which it hopes to add others, among which a plan for onshoring ultraviolet light lithography.<ref name="EcoGM">{{cite news |title=Peter Thiel doubles down on patriotism in the Trump era |url=https://www.economist.com/business/2025/04/20/peter-thiel-doubles-down-on-patriotism-in-the-trump-era |access-date=5 June 2025 |newspaper=The Economist |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250817001117/https://www.economist.com/business/2025/04/20/peter-thiel-doubles-down-on-patriotism-in-the-trump-era |archive-date= 2025-08-17}}</ref>

''Business Insider'' reports that, among Thiel's inner circle (who know well the billionaire's fondness for [[J. R. R. Tolkien|Tolkien's]] works), the fund is nicknamed "the Precious", in reference to [[the One Ring]] of Sauron.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Thomas |first1=Owen |title=Facebook Investor Peter Thiel Just Revealed What A Huge 'Lord Of The Rings' Fan He Is |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/peter-thiel-tolkien-2012-8 |access-date=5 June 2025 |work=Business Insider}}</ref>

In addition to Facebook, the Founders Fund made early-stage investments in numerous startups, including [[Airbnb]],<ref name=fortune/> Slide.com,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kirkpatrick |first=Marshall |date=2008-12-17 |title=PayPal Reunion: Dave McClure Joins Founders Fund |url=https://readwrite.com/paypal_reunion_dave_mcclure_jo/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190131145200/https://readwrite.com/2008/12/17/paypal_reunion_dave_mcclure_jo/ |access-date=2023-04-30 |website=ReadWrite |archive-date=31 January 2019 |url-status=usurped |language=en-US}}</ref> [[LinkedIn]],<ref name=fortune/> [[Friendster]],<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rivlin |first=Gary |date=2006-10-15 |title=How Friendster lost chance for a jackpot – Business – International Herald Tribune |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2006/10/15/business/worldbusiness/15iht-friend.3160940.html |access-date=2023-04-30 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190131093636/https://www.nytimes.com/2006/10/15/business/worldbusiness/15iht-friend.3160940.html |issn=0362-4331 |url-status=live |archive-date=31 January 2019 }}</ref> [[RapLeaf]], [[Geni.com]], [[Yammer]], [[Spotify]],<ref name=fortune/> [[Practice Fusion]], [[MetaMed]], Vator, [[SpaceX]],<ref name=fortune/> [[IronPort]], [[Votizen]], [[Asana (web application)|Asana]], [[Big Think]], [[CapLinked]], [[Nanotronics Imaging]], [[Powerset (company)|Powerset]] (Thiel is on the board of Nanotronics and Powerset<ref>{{cite web |title=Peter Andreas Thiel - PLTR {{!}} Palantir Technologies Inc. - Wall Street Journal |url=https://www.wsj.com/market-data/quotes/PLTR/company-people/executive-profile/43952425 |work=The Wall Street Journal |access-date=17 August 2025}}</ref>), [[Stripe (company)|Stripe]], Bullish Global (the parent company Block.one had also received investment from Thiel <ref>{{cite news |last1=Rooney |first1=Kate |title=Billionaire tech investor Peter Thiel bets on crypto start-up Block.one |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2018/07/16/billionaire-investor-peter-thiel-bets-on-crypto-start-up-blockone.html |access-date=17 August 2025 |work=CNBC |date=17 July 2018 |language=en}}</ref>), [[AltSchool]],<ref>{{Cite web |last=Adams |first=Susan |title=Can AltSchool—The Edtech Startup With $174M From Billionaires Like Zuckerberg And Thiel—Save Itself From Failure? |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/susanadams/2019/01/30/can-altschoolthe-edtech-startup-with-174m-from-billionaires-like-zuckerberg-and-thielsave-itself-from-failure/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190130233757/https://www.forbes.com/sites/susanadams/2019/01/30/can-altschoolthe-edtech-startup-with-174m-from-billionaires-like-zuckerberg-and-thielsave-itself-from-failure/#7f8cd5241997 |access-date=2023-04-30 |website=Forbes |archive-date=30 January 2019 |language=en}}</ref> [[Trade Republic]],<ref>{{cite news |title=The people with power at Trade Republic |url=https://sifted.eu/articles/people-with-power-trade-republic |access-date=31 July 2025 |work=Sifted}}</ref> [[PsiQuantum]] (photonic quantum computing),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lavine |first1=Robert |title=PsiQuantum crosses off $450m in funding - |url=https://globalventuring.com/corporate/psiquantum-crosses-off-450m-in-funding/ |date=28 July 2021}}</ref> and [[Rigetti Computing]] (superconducting quantum computing).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Quant |first1=The |title=What Happens To Rigetti (RGTI) Now That Its Founder, Chad Rigetti, Has Left The Quantum Company? Will It Thrive Or Even Survive? |url=https://quantumzeitgeist.com/what-happens-to-rigetti-now-that-its-founder-chad-rigetti-has-left-the-quantum-company-with-his-ename/ |date=30 January 2023}}</ref> Thiel also backed [[DeepMind]], a UK start-up that was acquired by Google in early 2014 for £400 million.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Shead|first1=Sam|title=How DeepMind convinced billionaire Peter Thiel to invest without moving the company to Silicon Valley|url=https://www.businessinsider.com/how-deepmind-convinced-peter-thiel-to-invest-outside-silicon-valley-2017-7|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200301201419/https://www.businessinsider.com/how-deepmind-convinced-peter-thiel-to-invest-outside-silicon-valley-2017-7|archive-date=1 March 2020|access-date=1 March 2020|publisher=Business Insider}}</ref> Founders Fund is an important backer of the Berlin-based platform [[ResearchGate]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kroker |first1=Michael |title=WirtschaftsWoche |url=https://www.wiwo.de/erfolg/gruender/researchgate-chef-ijad-madisch-da-ist-peter-thiel-aufgestanden-und-hat-gesagt-i-want-to-invest-in-this-guy/28959704.html |access-date=1 August 2025 |work=wiwo.de |date=3 February 2023}}</ref>

Since 2012, Founders Fund had been quietly buying bitcoin, with the investment totaling around $20&nbsp;million" (''[[Bloomberg News|Bloomberg]]'' remarked that, "the investment amounts to a rounding error for Thiel's firm.").<ref>{{cite news |last1=Chapman |first1=Lizette |title=Thiel's Founders Fund Has Been Quietly Buying Bitcoin Since 2012 |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2018-01-03/thiel-s-founders-fund-has-been-quietly-buying-bitcoin-since-2012 |url-status=live |work=Bloomberg.com |date=3 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180103033905/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2018-01-03/thiel-s-founders-fund-has-been-quietly-buying-bitcoin-since-2012 |archive-date=3 January 2018 |language=en}}</ref> The company sold the majority of its crypto holdings for $1.8 billion in March 2022 (with bitcoin making up two-thirds of its holdings), right before the market crashed.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Quiroz-Gutierrez |first1=Marco |title=Peter Thiel's Founders Fund generated more than $1 billion by offloading an 8-year Bitcoin bet just before markets soured |url=https://fortune.com/2023/01/18/peter-thiels-founders-fund-sold-crypto-just-before-markets-soured/ |work=Fortune |date=18 January 2023 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20260226155223/https://fortune.com/2023/01/18/peter-thiels-founders-fund-sold-crypto-just-before-markets-soured/ |url-status=live |archive-date=26 February 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

Also in 2017, Thiel was one of the first outside investors in [[Clearview AI]], a [[Facial recognition system|facial recognition]] technology startup that has raised concerns in the tech world and media for its risks of weaponization.<ref name=NYT2020-10-18>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/01/18/technology/clearview-privacy-facial-recognition.html|title=The Secretive Company That Might End Privacy as We Know It|last=Hill|first=Kashmir|date=18 January 2020|work=The New York Times|access-date=18 January 2020|language=en-US|issn=0362-4331|archive-date=18 January 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200118102009/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/01/18/technology/clearview-privacy-facial-recognition.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2024, alongside [[General Catalyst]] and Red Cell Partners, the Founders Fund incubated the defense incubator Valinor Enterprises. The co-founders are former Palantir's senior vice president Julie Bush (CEO), [[Trae Stephens]], General Catalyst's Paul Kwan and Red Cell's Grant Verstandig.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Demarest |first1=Colin |title=Exclusive: Valinor wants to build defense-tech 'picks and shovels' |url=https://www.axios.com/2025/10/15/valinor-harbor-bush-stephens-kwan |work=Axios |date=15 October 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

In 2024, the Founders Fund led a massive US$85 million seed round for the UAE's decentralized, open-source AI platform SentientAGI (Sentient Foundation), which seeks to challenge Perplexity and OpenAI's closed models as well as handle the problem of AI proliferation, which concentrates power in the hands of large players like Google and Amazon.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Only |first1=Subscription |title=Peter Thiel-backed AI Startup Sentient Pitches Itself More Transparent than OpenAI and Perplexity |url=https://www.adweek.com/media/peter-thiel-backed-ai-startup-sentient-pitches-itself-more-transparent-than-openai-and-perplexity/ |url-status=live |access-date=7 August 2025 |date=2 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250519041346/https://www.adweek.com/media/peter-thiel-backed-ai-startup-sentient-pitches-itself-more-transparent-than-openai-and-perplexity/ |archive-date=19 May 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Peter Thiel's Fund Leads $85M Seed Round in Sentient AI – ACU |url=https://acudc.com/peter-thiels-fund-leads-85m-seed-round-in-sentient-ai/ |access-date=7 August 2025}}</ref> In 2024 it also invested in Impulse Space (in-space transportation services),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Oxford |first1=Clarence |title=Impulse Space secures $150M in Series B funding to drive growth |url=https://www.spacedaily.com/reports/Impulse_Space_secures_150M_in_Series_B_funding_to_drive_growth_999.html |access-date=10 August 2025 |work=Space Daily}}</ref> Ramp,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Azevedo |first1=Mary Ann |title=Ramp raises another $150M co-led by Khosla and Founders Fund at a $7.65B valuation |url=https://techcrunch.com/2024/04/17/ramp-raises-another-150-million-co-led-by-khosla-founders-fund-at-a-7-65b-valuation/ |access-date=10 August 2025 |work=TechCrunch |date=17 April 2024}}</ref> Crusoe (AI infrastructure using clean energy).<ref>{{cite news |title=Crusoe secures $600 million in Series D round led by Founders Fund |url=https://www.reuters.com/technology/crusoe-secures-600-million-series-d-round-led-by-founders-fund-2024-12-12 |access-date=10 August 2025 |date=12 March 2026 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/QxeRG |url-status=live |archive-date=13 December 2024}}</ref>

In 2025, the Founders Fund invests in Erebor (a new digital bank which is founded by Palmer Luckey and has quickly reached a US$2 billion valuation),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hornstein |first1=Julia |title=Anduril CEO Palmer Luckey's new digital banking startup set to be valued at $2 billion, sources say |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/palmer-luckeys-new-digital-bank-erebor-valuation-2025-7 |access-date=31 July 2025 |work=Business Insider}}</ref> EnduroSat (Bulgarian startup that produces Gen3 satellites at scale),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bock |first1=Greg |title=EnduroSat announces €43 Million Investment to Scale Gen3 Satellite Production |url=https://spaceinsider.tech/2025/05/28/endurosat-announces-e43-million-investment-to-scale-gen3-satellite-production/ |access-date=10 August 2025 |work=Space Insider |date=28 May 2025}}</ref> [[Varda Space Industries|Varda]] (space medicine and hypersonic testbed startup, founded by the Thiel Fellow Delian Asparouhov, Trae Stephens and [[Will Bruey]], and incubated at the Founders Fund),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Shieber |first1=Jonathan |title=Space manufacturing startup Varda, incubated at Founders Fund, emerges with $9 million in funding |url=https://techcrunch.com/2020/12/08/space-manufacturing-startup-varda-incubated-at-founders-fund-emerges-with-9-million-in-funding/ |access-date=10 August 2025 |work=TechCrunch |date=9 December 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Zaveri |first1=Paayal |date=10 July 2025 |title=Varda Raises 187 Million to Manufacture Drugs In Space |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-07-10/varda-raises-187-million-to-manufacture-drugs-in-space |url-access=subscription |access-date=10 August 2025}}</ref> [[Hadrian Automation]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Subin |first1=Samantha |title=Defense manufacturing startup Hadrian closes $260 million funding round led by Peter Thiel's Founders Fund |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2025/07/17/hadrian-funding-round-thiel-founders-fund.html |access-date=10 August 2025 |work=CNBC |date=17 July 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

In 2026, the fund co-led investments in Amsterdam-based defense startup Onodrim (launched by Palantir alumni) and AI startup Arda (led by former OpenAI executive Bob McGrew), both of which bear Tolkien-themed names.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ludlow |first1=Edward |title=Palantir Alumni Launch Thiel-Backed European Defense Startup |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-02-13/palantir-alumni-launch-thiel-backed-european-defense-startup |date=13 February 2026}}</ref>
<ref>{{cite news |last1=Clark |first1=Meghan Bobrowsky and Kate |title=OpenAI's Former Research Chief Aims to Automate Manufacturing With AI |url=https://www.wsj.com/tech/ai/openais-former-research-chief-aims-to-automate-manufacturing-with-ai-8871f265 |work=The Wall Street Journal |date=4 March 2026}}</ref> It also backed the ocean data center startup Panthalassa, with Thiel leading the round personally.
<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rogelberg |first1=Sasha |title=Peter Thiel is leading investment in an ocean data center powered by waves—and the startup is reportedly worth $1 billion |url=https://fortune.com/2026/05/14/peter-thiel-leading-investment-ocean-data-center-panthalassa/ |work=Fortune |date=14 May 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

====Anduril Industries====
{{Main|Anduril Industries}}

Anduril is a defense hardware startup that was founded in 2017 by [[Palmer Luckey]] (who is Thiel's mentee), Joe Chen, [[Trae Stephens]], Matt Grimm and Brian Schimpf (the latter three are Palantir and Founders Fund alumni).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hartung |first1=William D. |title=Venture Capitalists Are Gunning for New Frontiers in Techno-War |url=https://www.thenation.com/article/archive/artificial-intelligence-techno-war/ |date=26 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251127145554/https://www.thenation.com/article/archive/artificial-intelligence-techno-war/ |url-status=live |archive-date= 2025-11-27}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Stokes |first1=Samantha |last2=Hornstein |first2=Julia |last3=Russell |title=Meet the new tech mafia in town: the Palantir Mafia |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/palantir-mafia-former-employees-startups-anduril-2025-3 |url-status=live |work=Business Insider |date=17 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260212035439/https://www.businessinsider.com/palantir-mafia-former-employees-startups-anduril-2025-3 |archive-date= 2026-02-12}}</ref> The Founders Fund backed the company since its inception, leading its 2017 seed round. The $1 billion (part of the $2.5 billion Series G funding round) the Founders Fund invested in Anduril in June 2025 was the largest investment in the fund's history.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bort |first1=Julie |title=Anduril in talks to raise up to $2.5B at $28B valuation |url=https://techcrunch.com/2025/02/07/anduril-in-talks-to-raise-up-to-2-5b-at-28b-valuation/ |work=TechCrunch |date=7 February 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Bort |first1=Julie |title=Anduril raises $2.5B at $30.5B valuation led by Founders Fund |url=https://techcrunch.com/2025/06/05/anduril-raises-2-5b-at-30-5b-valuation-led-by-founders-fund/ |work=TechCrunch |date=5 June 2025}}</ref> In December 2024, it was reported that Palantir and Anduril were forming a consortium of new generation defense companies (which included [[SpaceX]], [[OpenAI]], [[Scale AI]], Saronic Technologies and others) that they would lead in order to challenge the dominance of traditional defense companies.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Albon |first1=Courtney |title=Defense tech firms establish AI-focused consortium |url=https://www.defensenews.com/pentagon/2024/12/06/defense-tech-firms-establish-ai-focused-consortium/ |work=Defense News |date=6 December 2024 |language=en}}</ref> In July 2025, when the Trump administration's budget bill included a provision that required border surveillance towers to be "tested and accepted by U.S. Customs and Border Protection to deliver autonomous capabilities", Edith Olmsted of ''[[The New Republic]]'' made the criticism that the government favoured Peter Thiel's business, as the provision (which only Anduril fulfilled) effectively ruled out Anduril's rivals, namely [[General Dynamics]] and the Israeli-based [[Elbit Systems]].<ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Olmsted |first1=Edith |title=Trump Sneaked Huge Gift for Peter Thiel–Backed Company Into Budget |url=https://newrepublic.com/post/197779/donald-trump-peter-thiel-budget-anduril-surveillance |magazine=The New Republic |date=10 July 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Kinder |first1=Tabby |last2=Hammond |first2=George |title=Palantir and Anduril join forces with tech groups to bid for Pentagon contracts |url=https://www.ft.com/content/6cfdfe2b-6872-4963-bde8-dc6c43be5093 |work=Financial Times |date=22 December 2024 |url-access=subscription}}</ref>

====General Matter====
{{Main|General Matter}}

General Matter emerged from stealth in April 2025, focusing on "production and handling of High-Assay, Low-Enriched Uranium (HALEU)."<ref>{{cite news |title=US uranium enrichment startup emerges from stealth |url=https://www.world-nuclear-news.org/articles/us-uranium-enrichment-startup-emerges-from-stealth |access-date=23 April 2025 |work=World Nuclear News |date=23 April 2025 |language=en}}</ref> Scott Nolan, who formerly worked for SpaceX, left the Founders Fund to focus on the company and become the CEO, Thiel is also on the company's board of directors, which is noted by the ''Economist'' to be a rare occurrence these days.<ref name="EcoGM"/> The plant in Paducah, built by General Matter, will be the "first privately developed US uranium enrichment facility".<ref>{{cite web |last1=Operle |first1=Derek |title=Billionaire Peter Thiel backing first privately developed US uranium enrichment facility in Paducah |url=https://www.wkms.org/energy/2025-07-25/billionaire-peter-thiel-backing-first-privately-developed-us-uranium-enrichment-facility-in-paducah |website=WKMS |access-date=26 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251226070749/https://www.wkms.org/energy/2025-07-25/billionaire-peter-thiel-backing-first-privately-developed-us-uranium-enrichment-facility-in-paducah |url-status=live |language=en |date=25 July 2025|archive-date= 2025-12-26}}</ref>

